Z||000000||KnightRyder31||08-14-2000||11:32 PM||||Man!!! My Fiero is having some problems! Now, I completly lost my passing gear! Also, my gas pedal became quite sloppy. Another problem: When I floor it, and I hit about about 28MPH, my car just turns into a dog, so slow! Its like my Fiero is dying!! I took it to a mechanic, and he said that is was some cable. And its going to cost $100-$300 to fix!!! WTF!? ||||reg||13
Z||000001||Greg Piet||08-15-2000||12:24 AM||||100-300 for a cable!??!!?!?!?!?!?!<P>WTF is right!!   waht cable is it?   maybe someone here can help!||||reg||1
Z||000002||Ed||08-15-2000||12:39 AM||||Cable?<P>Open your trunk and work the throttle directly.  Does the engine cut out?  Sounds more like a tune-up problem than any cable.<P>There's only a throttle cable, a cruise control cable (not likely), a parking brake cable (!), and two shifting cables if it is a manual.  <P>Check your throttle and then come back and we can speculate on your tune-up problem. I'd guess fuel or exhaust (fuel filter, catalytic converter, air filter, etc.).<P>Or, maybe he's right and your throttle cable has come loose or stretched.  ||||reg||1
Z||000003||StuGood||08-15-2000||01:01 AM||||The only cable that makes any sense to me, would be the TV cable.  Kind of like cable TV, except you don't have to pay for it every month  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">.<P>Actually, the TV (throttle valve) cable is the thing that tells the auto transmission how far you have stomped the accelerator pedal.  It uses this info to help decide when (and if) to shift gears.<P>I'm certainly not an expert on automatic transmissions, but I believe the TV cable's operation is critical to the transmission's  performance.  If your TV cable needs to be replaced / adjusted, you can probably do it yourself for <B>much</B>less than $100 - basically, whatever the cost of the replacement cable is.  <P>It isn't difficult, because the cable just goes from the throttle bracket, to an opening on top of the transmission housing - you can do it while standing behind the car.  You don't have to get under the car or anything.  Believe that the Haynes manual outlines the procedure.            <P>The above assumes that the shop knows what it's talking about, and it's just the cable.  If your intermediate (passing) gear is really out, you might need to change an actuator pin - which can be accessed by removing the pan.  But, if the intermediate band is completely worn out - well, try the cable first.  Good luck  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> ! ||||reg||5
Z||000004||triker||08-15-2000||05:13 PM||||If it's a worn intermediate band, it will still kick down, but will drop into 1st gear and promptly hit the rev limiter.  The TV cable affects the shift points, but I don't see how it could affect engine performance.  Please post what you finally come up with.||||reg||1
Z||000005||TooLowFiero||08-15-2000||05:43 PM||||i always thought of it as a Kickdown Cable  but who knows  <BR>anyways i disconnected mine beuse it was not letting my throttle open up to full bore  my tranny shifts better now than it ever did and it sometimes sqauks the tires into second<BR>mind that this is a 123000 mile iron duck never rebuilt needs a valve cover gasket get crappy gas mileage i get about 210 miles per tank and i can still spin the tires on pavement wierd huh  darn those ole 3 speed autos<P>Bobby aka TooLow<BR>85 se 2.8 4 speed<BR>86 2m4 auto<BR>88 coupe w/90 grand am getrag =o)||||reg||1
Z||000006||contra||08-15-2000||05:59 PM||||while your at it too, on my automatic i modified my cable so that it's even more sensitive. it has almost no lag now switching gears if i step on it, and that's the way i like it  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><BR>||||reg||1
Z||000007||Raydar||08-16-2000||12:07 AM||||I'm with Stu and the other TV (kickdown) cable folks. I'm guessing that without the cable, the trans is shifting into 2nd and high gears very quickly. Could make your car very slow, and would lug the engine. Try shifting it manually, and see if it feels better. <BR>Had an ex g.f. with an Iroc. Her TV cable was left disconnected (by the dealer). With the gas floored, it would shift into high gear at about 25 MPH. Very soft shift. Almost sounded like it was slipping. Reconnecting the cable fixed it.<BR>Good luck. <P>------------------<BR>Raydar<BR>88 Formula T-Top <BR>88 Coupe (ISO big V-6)<P>When I die, I want to go peacefully, in my sleep, like Grandpa.<BR>Not screaming, like the passengers in his car.||||reg||1
Z||000008||theogre||08-16-2000||09:22 PM||||No, It's not a "kickdown cable". Unlike a kickdown which only shifts the tranny down a gear, the TV controls line pressure and works with the internal governer to control shift timing. The TH125 will not work correctly without it, and it's adjustment is fairly critical. <P>The fact one person is apparantly running ok without it is definatly not normal. The TV should not normally keep the throttle from opening to WOT unless something is wrong in either the tranny or the cable.||||reg||1
Z||000009||KnightRyder31||08-23-2000||06:44 PM||||Ok, now I'm pissed!!! My car just died!Now, I can't even turn on my lights, or my cd player...no power, outta know where. When I go to start it, its like the battery is disconnected...but its not. Maybe the whole problem is the Computer. I dunno. I heard it was the CPU, and someone just told me that it could be some fuse problem. I'm thinking it's the CPU, because that might explain all of the other problems, right? How hard is it to replace the computer, and how much money do I need? <BR>P.S.<BR>Sorry about the lack of posts latley, I've been kicked out of my house for almost 2 weeks now. I'm staying at one of my friends house. I'll try to post more.<BR>Knight-||||reg||8
